===.description:
DIV{HEADER1{===,  -- strict equality}, UL{LI{DL{class => element, DT{Usage: }, DD{CODE{x === y, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, LI{Inputs:, UL{LI{SPAN{TT{x}}}, LI{SPAN{TT{y}}}}}, LI{Outputs:, UL{LI{SPAN{SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: Boolean, Boolean value}}, , , whether the expressions , TT{x},  and , TT{y},  are strictly equal}}}}}, DIV{HEADER2{Description}, PARA{Strictly equal expressions have the same type, so , TT{0===0.},  and , TT{0===0/1},  are false; the three types involved here are , TO{Macaulay2Doc :: ZZ}, , , TO{Macaulay2Doc :: RR}, , and , TO{Macaulay2Doc :: QQ}, .}, PARA{If x and y are , TO{Macaulay2Doc :: isMutable},  then they are strictly equal only if they are identical (i.e., at the same address in memory).  For details about why strict equality cannot depend on the contents of mutable hash tables, see , TO{Macaulay2Doc :: hashing}, .  On the other hand, if x and y are non-mutable, then they are strictly equal if and only if all their contents are strictly equal.}, TABLE{class => examples, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i1 : {1,2,3} === {1,2,3}

o1 = true, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i2 : {1,2,3} === {2,1,3}

o2 = false, class => language-macaulay2}}}}}, PARA{For some types, such as ring elements and matrices, strict equality is the same as mathematical equality. This tends to be the case for objects for which not much computation is not required to test equality. For certain other types, such as , TO{Macaulay2Doc :: Ideal},  or , TO{Macaulay2Doc :: Module}, , where extensive computations may be required, the operator , TO{Macaulay2Doc :: ==},  implements the desired comparison.}, TABLE{class => examples, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i3 : R = QQ[a..d];, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i4 : a^2+b === b+a^2

o4 = true, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i5 : ideal(a^2+b,c*d) === ideal(b+a^2,c*d+b+a^2)

o5 = false, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i6 : matrix{{a,b,c}} === matrix{{a,b,c}}

o6 = true, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i7 : matrix{{a,b,c}} === transpose matrix{{a},{b},{c}}

o7 = false, class => language-macaulay2}}}}}, PARA{As it happens, polynomial rings are mutable objects, and new ones are easily created, which are distinct from each other. For example, the rings , TT{A},  and , TT{B},  below are not strictly equal.}, TABLE{class => examples, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i8 : A = QQ[x]; B = QQ[x];, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i10 : A === B

o10 = false, class => language-macaulay2}}}}}}, DIV{HEADER2{See also}, UL{LI{TOH{Macaulay2Doc :: ==}}, LI{TOH{Macaulay2Doc :: =!=}}, LI{TOH{Macaulay2Doc :: operators}}}}, DIV{DIV{class => waystouse, HEADER2{Ways to use , TT{symbol ===}, :}, UL{LI{KBD{Thing === Thing}}}}, DIV{class => waystouse, HEADER2{For the programmer}, PARA{The object , TO{Macaulay2Doc :: ===},  is , SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: Keyword, keyword}}, .}}, HR{}, DIV{class => waystouse, PARA{The source of this document is in , TT{Macaulay2Doc/operators/equality.m2:50:0}, .}}}}

accumulate.description:
DIV{HEADER1{accumulate,  -- apply a binary operator repeatedly}, UL{LI{DL{class => element, DT{Usage: }, DD{CODE{accumulate(f, x, L), class => language-macaulay2}}, DD{CODE{accumulate(f, L), class => language-macaulay2}}, DD{CODE{accumulate(L, x, f), class => language-macaulay2}}, DD{CODE{accumulate(L, f), class => language-macaulay2}}}}, LI{Inputs:, UL{LI{SPAN{TT{f}, , , SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: Function, function}}, , }}, LI{SPAN{TT{x}, , , SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: Thing, thing}}, , }}, LI{SPAN{TT{L}, , , SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: VisibleList, visible list}}, , , TEX{or an instance of a class with the }, TO{Macaulay2Doc :: iterator}, TEX{ method installed}}}}}, LI{Outputs:, UL{LI{SPAN{TT{M}, , , SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: List, list}}, , }}}}}, DIV{HEADER2{Description}, DIV{PARA{TEX{Suppose , TT{L=\{x0, x1, ..., xn\}}, . Then for any binary operator , TT{f}, , , TT{accumulate(f, L)},  returns the list , TT{\{f(x0, x1), f(f(x0, x1), x2), ...\} }, . In other words, the binary operator is applied to the first two elements of , TT{L}, , then to that result along with the next unused element of , TT{L}, , and so forth.}}}, TABLE{class => examples, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i1 : accumulate(plus, {0,1,2,3,4,5})

o1 = {1, 3, 6, 10, 15}

o1 : List, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i2 : accumulate(concatenate, {a,b,c,d,e})

o2 = {ab, abc, abcd, abcde}

o2 : List, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i3 : accumulate((i,j) -> i|j|i, {"a","b","c","d","e"})

o3 = {aba, abacaba, abacabadabacaba, abacabadabacabaeabacabadabacaba}

o3 : List, class => language-macaulay2}}}}}, DIV{PARA{TEX{If , TT{accumulate(f, x, L)},  is called, the element , TT{x},  is used as the first argument of the binary function , TT{f}, . In other words, , TT{accumulate(f, \{x0, x1, \ldots, xn\})},  is equivalent to , TT{accumulate(f, x0, \{x1, \ldots, xn\})}, .}}}, TABLE{class => examples, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i4 : accumulate(plus, 0, {1,2,3,4,5})

o4 = {1, 3, 6, 10, 15}

o4 : List, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i5 : accumulate((x, y) -> x^y, 2, {3,2,1,2})

o5 = {8, 64, 64, 4096}

o5 : List, class => language-macaulay2}}}}}, DIV{PARA{TEX{The function , TT{accumulate(\{x_0, x_1, \ldots, x_n\}, f)},  returns the list , TT{\{..., f(x_{n-2}, f(x_{n-1}, x_n)), f(x_{n-1}, x_n) \} }, . That is, , TT{f},  is applied to the last two elements of the list, and the result placed at the end of the output. Then the accumulation proceeds backwards through the list. The optional argument , TT{x},  in , TT{accumulate(L, x, f)},  is used as the second argument in the first evaluation of , TT{f}, . So , TT{accumulate(\{x_0, x_1, \ldots, x_{n-1}\}, x_n, f)},  is equivalent to , TT{accumulate(\{x_0, x_1, \ldots, x_n\}, f)}, .}}}, TABLE{class => examples, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i6 : accumulate({a,b,c,d,e}, concatenate)

o6 = {abcde, bcde, cde, de}

o6 : List, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i7 : accumulate({a,b,c,d}, e, concatenate)

o7 = {abcde, bcde, cde, de}

o7 : List, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i8 : accumulate({2,3,2,1}, 2, (x, y) -> x^y)

o8 = {512, 9, 2, 1}

o8 : List, class => language-macaulay2}}}}}, DIV{PARA{TEX{If }, TT{L}, TEX{ is an instance of class the the }, TO{Macaulay2Doc :: iterator}, TEX{ method installed, e.g., a string, then it may also be used with }, TT{accumulate}, TEX{, but only the versions with }, TT{f}, TEX{ as the first argument.  Its return value in this case is an }, TO{Macaulay2Doc :: Iterator}, TEX{ object.}}}, TABLE{class => examples, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i9 : iter = accumulate(identity, "abcde")

o9 = iter

o9 : Iterator, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i10 : next iter

o10 = (a, b)

o10 : Sequence, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i11 : next iter

o11 = ((a, b), c)

o11 : Sequence, class => language-macaulay2}}}}, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i12 : next iter

o12 = (((a, b), c), d)

o12 : Sequence, class => language-macaulay2}}}}}, DIV{PARA{TEX{The difference between , TT{fold},  and }, TO{Macaulay2Doc :: accumulate}, TEX{ is that , TT{fold},  returns the final result of all the nested evaluations of , TT{f}, , while , TT{accumulate},  lists all the intermediate values as well.}}}, TABLE{class => examples, TR{TD{PRE{CODE{i13 : fold({2,3,2,1}, 2, (x,y) -> x^y)

o13 = 512, class => language-macaulay2}}}}}}, DIV{HEADER2{See also}, UL{LI{TOH{Macaulay2Doc :: apply}}, LI{TOH{Macaulay2Doc :: fold}}, LI{TOH{Macaulay2Doc :: lists and sequences}}}}, DIV{DIV{class => waystouse, HEADER2{Ways to use , TT{accumulate}, :}, UL{LI{KBD{accumulate(Function,Thing)}}, LI{KBD{accumulate(Function,Thing,Thing)}}, LI{KBD{accumulate(Function,Thing,VisibleList)}}, LI{KBD{accumulate(Function,VisibleList)}}, LI{KBD{accumulate(VisibleList,Function)}}, LI{KBD{accumulate(VisibleList,Thing,Function)}}}}, DIV{class => waystouse, HEADER2{For the programmer}, PARA{The object , TO{Macaulay2Doc :: accumulate},  is , SPAN{a , TO2{Macaulay2Doc :: MethodFunction, method function}}, .}}, HR{}, DIV{class => waystouse, PARA{The source of this document is in , TT{Macaulay2Doc/functions/accumulate-doc.m2:78:0}, .}}}}

